e hill/bush/cloud scenery or the ground. Hmm, but those are header-driven (scenery set). The header byte at 0x268E ($50) controls the terrain/scenery; changing the header is arguably a \"layout byte\" but the task says \"the object changed\".\n\nHmm, what about the ground height? The ground is drawn from a \"background\" object at the start.\n\nActually, maybe the first object byte 0x07 IS the \"blank sky\" object, and the second byte 0x81 begins the ground? If I change 0x07's high nibble to something like 0x8?, blocks appear.\n\nLet me just try experiment A and see the result. If it doesn't produce a clean visible change, I'll iterate.\n\nActually — better idea to make a *reliable* and *attributable* proof: change the byte so that the ? block row becomes a brick row, and scroll to it. But identifying that byte requires decoding.\n\nLet me try to decode the object stream properly using the decoder code. Let me re-read the decoder $9595 semantics:\n\nIt's called with the parser state. It reads a byte from the stream and computes a metatile value ($07) to place. Special values:\n- low nibble $F → \"nothing/gap\"\n- low nibble $C → solid block?\n- low nibble $D → ? block?\n- low nibble $E → pipe?\n\nHmm. And the high nibble = object type/height.\n\nHmm, actually I recall the SMB object format from the \"SMB level data\" doc by \"Dahrkdaiz\":\n- Object byte: high nibble = object type, low nibble = \"number of columns - 1\"? \n- Types:\n  - 0x0: nothing\n  - 0x1: bridge? \n  ...\nHmm.\n\nLet me just experiment. It's cheaper.\n\nExperiment A: write prgRom 0x2690 = 0x67, reset, start, screenshot.\n\nWait, but the current emulator state: attract demo playing. The hack switch didn't reset. Let's do the write then reset.\n\nwrite_memory address 9872 (0x2690), bytes [0x67], mem_type prgRom, reason.\n\nThen reset_emulator, then play_input to start 1-1, then screenshot.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nActually hold on — before that, let me reconsider: the first screen's *scenery* (hill/bush/cloud) comes from the header/scenery objects; the ground from the terrain. The \"object\" the task refers to could be the ? block. But any visible change is fine.\n\nHmm, one concern: changing the very first object byte may cause the parser to misread the whole stream, producing a garbage level — which still proves the routine read the byte (the level changed). The task says \"showing the object changed as the routine would read it\" — a garbage level is a legitimate demonstration but less elegant. Let me aim for something cleaner.\n\nLet me try to identify the object for the ground row. Hmm.\n\nActually, let's use the *live* approach to decode: I can write to prgRom while the game is in the level, then trigger a level reload via death (the parser re-reads the stream). But the level is loaded at Start; death reload re-runs LoadAreaData and the parser. So: \n- Write prgRom 0x2690 = X live.\n- Then make Mario die (hold right into Goomba) → after the card, level reloads → parser reads the modified byte.\nThat avoids reset+start. But we still need to see.\n\nSimplest: reset+start.\n\nLet me proceed with experiment A.\n\nActually, let me be smarter and use the standard SMB 1-1 data knowledge. Let me search my memory of the 1-1 object list from the disassembly:\n\n```\nAreaData1_1:\n      .db $50, $21, $07, $81, $47, $24, $57, $00, $63, $01, $77, $01, $C9, $71, $68, $F2, $E7, $73, $97, $FB, $06, $83, $5C, $01, $D7, $22, $E7, $00, $03, $A7, $6C, $02, $B3, $22, $E3, $01, $E7, $07, $47, $A0, $57, $06, $A7, $01, $D3, $00, $D7, $01, $07, $81, ...\n```\n\nHmm, I've definitely seen this. The format: each object is TWO bytes? Let's test: pairs (07,81),(47,24),(57,00),(63,01),(77,01),(C9,71),(68,F2),(E7,73),(97,FB),(06,83),(5C,01),(D7,22),(E7,00),(03,A7),(6C,02),(B3,22),(E3,01),(E7,07),(47,A0),(57,06),(A7,01),(D3,00),(D7,01),(07,81),(67,20),...\n\nHmm, if each object is 2 bytes with the second being the \"metatile/terrain\" and the first the \"scenery\"... In SMB, the \"background scenery\" (bushes, clouds, hills) is encoded in one byte and the terrain (blocks) in another; each object writes a column of 13 metatiles.\n\nActually!! I remember now: SMB objects are encoded in a special way:\n- The level data is a sequence of bytes. Each \"object\" is a single byte in {0x00-0x7F} or a 2-byte pair starting with a byte >= 0x80? No...\n\nThe patter","truncated":true}
