r is only updated when the screen scrolls. At level start, the buffer might be pre-filled by the \"initial screen\" routine from the header alone (the header's \"background\" and the ground). Then the visible hill/bush/cloud... hmm, they must come from data.\n\nUnless the hill/bush/cloud are part of the **header's scenery byte** at $A68F (0x21)! The header byte 1 ($A68F=0x21) bits 4-5 = 2 → \"scenery set 2\"? Hmm, and the hill/bush/cloud layout comes from... no.\n\nHmm, actually! In SMB, the level header's byte 0 low bits might select a \"**background/terrain metatile set**\", and the *scenery* (hill/bush/cloud) is drawn by the parser from the object stream. The first screen's scenery must come from objects.\n\nOK, radical idea: let me check whether the *whole* level data even matters: blank/garble a large chunk. Let me write 0xFD over $A692-$A6B1 (32 bytes) and see. If nothing changes, then the visible 1-1 is built from somewhere else entirely (or the game caches the level in SRAM? no).\n\nHmm, actually, hold on! Maybe the game reads the level data and *copies* it into RAM at $6000 (SRAM) or elsewhere at boot, and the parser reads from RAM? But the trace showed reads in CPU space $A692 which is ROM. So the parser reads ROM.\n\nUnless... those reads at $A692 were from a DIFFERENT context (not the level parser) — e.g., the enemy spawner reading a table at $A692? But $E7/$E8 = $A690 and the PCs were $9514/$959F/$95E7 etc. — the parser we identified. So the parser reads $A692.\n\nSo why doesn't changing $A692-$A699 change screen 1? Because those bytes might be for screens that come later in the demo. The demo started at level start; the parser's initial build (screen 1) might use bytes that are... hmm.\n\nWAIT. Maybe the parser reads $A692 first because the demo *resumed* mid-level? The attract demo might start at the level start though.\n\nHmm, let me test the timing differently: after reset, the demo takes ~400+ frames. The reads at frame 439 read $A692. If that's the initial build, screen 1. If the demo starts at frame 400 and Mario immediately walks, by frame 439 he's at x~50 (still screen 1). So reads at 439 = screen 1 build. Then blanking $A692 should change screen 1.\n\nUnless the demo load uses a *saved* level state and doesn't rebuild from ROM until scrolling... Hmm! That's possible: the attract demo might have been \"recorded\" and when it starts, the game loads 1-1 normally (LoadAreaData + parser). Hmm.\n\nHold on — maybe in my reset+start test, the screen I'm looking at is NOT built from the ROM at all but from the level data *buffer* that persists? No, reset clears RAM.\n\nHmm, is the level data maybe in **CHR/RAM copied from ROM at boot**? No.\n\nLet me try a much more aggressive test: overwrite $A692-$A6D1 (64 bytes) with 0xFD and see. Actually let me overwrite a large chunk $A690-$A6CF with 0x00. If screen 1 is unchanged, something's very wrong.\n\nHmm, but 0x00 might mean \"blank\" and the scenery... let me use a clearly-bogus pattern: 0xFF repeated. 0xFF = type F, len F. Might build weird stuff.\n\nLet me do $A690-$A6CF (64 bytes) = 0xFF. Reset+start+screenshot.\n\nActually, wait! I want to first double-check something: does the game even re-read the ROM after reset_emulator, or does the emulator keep a *loaded ROM image* and reset just restarts? It should re-run the code and read the patched ROM. Fine.\n\nHmm, let me also consider: maybe `write_memory prgRom` writes to the ROM but the CPU reads via a *mirror* that's cached... but read_memory cpu at $A690 returned 0s, matching. So the CPU sees the patched bytes.\n\nSo the parser sees patched bytes. Yet the level doesn't change. => The visible first screen isn't built from those bytes.\n\n=> The visible first screen must be built from *other* bytes. Which? Maybe the level data for 1-1 that the game actually uses is at a DIFFERENT address than $A690, and $E7/$E8=$A690 is used for something else (e.g., enemy/objects) while the terrain comes from another pointer.\n\nThe mapping says the layout pointer table $9D2C/$9D4E resolved 1-1 to $A68E header / $A690 records, and proved it by a ledgered write that made 1-1 load a different level (screenshot showed a completely different level). So $E7/$E8 IS the layout pointer. Hmm, and that write at 0x1D34 changed $E7","truncated":true}
